-- title:
--   Triviality of the Gal(L/ℚ)-action on the toric part
-- statement:
--   **Arithmetic setting.** Fixed are a prime $p$, a natural number $M$ with $5 \le M$ and $p \nmid M$, a field $L$ of characteristic zero which is a cyclotomic extension of $\mathbb{Q}$ of type $\{p\}$, and a primitive $p$-th root of unity $\zeta \in L$. Further, $K$ is an intermediate field of $L \subseteq \operatorname{LaurentSeries} L$ with $K =$ [`ModularCurve.laurentBaseChange L (ModularCurve.x1FunctionField (M * p))`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L103), that is, the $L$-subfield of $L((q))$ generated by the coefficientwise image of the function field of $X_1(Mp)$ over $\mathbb{Q}$; $A$ is a discrete valuation domain with $L$ as fraction field, such that $p$ lies in the maximal ideal of $A$ and $\zeta$ lies in the image of $A \to L$, and $K$ is an $A$-algebra compatibly with $A \to L \to K$. An element $j \in K$ is given whose Laurent expansion is [`ModularCurve.coeffEmb L ModularCurve.jq`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L81), the $q$-expansion of the modular invariant pushed into $L((q))$, with $j \ne 0$. Finally $k$ is an algebraically closed field of characteristic $p$ which is an $A$-algebra. Throughout, $X :=$ [`ModularCurve.TwoChartModel A K j`](def/ModularCurve_TwoChartModel.html#L229) (the pushout of the two chart morphisms) with structure morphism $x :=$ [`ModularCurve.TwoChart.modelTo A K j`](def/ModularCurve_TwoChartModel.html#L252) to $\operatorname{Spec} A$, which is assumed proper; [`ModularCurve.TwoChart.ιFin A K j`](def/ModularCurve_TwoChartModel.html#L231) denotes the finite chart $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the subalgebra `chartAlgFin A K j` $=$ `chartAlg A K {j}` of $K$.
--
--   **The special fibre as a union of two curves.** $C_1, C_2$ are schemes with morphisms $c_1, c_2$ to $\operatorname{Spec} k$, each proper, smooth of relative dimension $1$ and geometrically integral, together with closed immersions $i_1 : C_1 \to X_k$, $i_2 : C_2 \to X_k$ over $k$, where $X_k$ is the fibre product of $x$ with $\operatorname{Spec} k \to \operatorname{Spec} A$. The hypothesis `hcover` states that every point of $X_k$ lies in the image of $i_1$ or of $i_2$; `hred` that the fibre product of $i_1$ and $i_2$ is reduced, and `hn`, `hn0` that its number of points equals $n$ with $0 < n$. Sections $\varepsilon$ of $x$ over $\operatorname{Spec} A$, and $\varepsilon_1$ of $c_1$, $\varepsilon_2$ of $c_2$ over $\operatorname{Spec} k$ are given, with `hε₁` asserting that $\varepsilon_1$ followed by $i_1$ is the base change of $\varepsilon$ to $k$.
--
--   **Relative $\operatorname{Pic}^0$ data.** $D$ is a `RelativePic0Designation` for $x$, i.e. a scheme $D.P$ with a structure morphism $D.\mathrm{toBase}$ to $\operatorname{Spec} A$ and a zero section; `hrep` asserts that $D$ represents, with respect to $\varepsilon$, the sub-Picard condition `algEquivZeroCut` (rigidified line bundles on $X$ satisfying `FibrewiseAlgEquivZero`): it carries a Poincaré rigidified bundle satisfying the condition, every such bundle over any base is classified by a unique morphism to $D.\mathrm{toBase}$, and the zero section classifies the unit bundle. Moreover $D.\mathrm{toBase}$ is smooth (`hsm`) and separated (`hsep`). The hypothesis `hreps` is the corresponding representability statement for the base change $D_k$ and the curve $X_k$ with section the base change of $\varepsilon$, and `hPk` asserts that its Poincaré bundle is isomorphic to the base change to $k$ of the pullback of the Poincaré bundle of `hrep.some` along the first projection of $D_k$. Similarly $D_1, D_2$ are designations for $c_1, c_2$ represented, via `hrep₁`, `hrep₂`, with respect to $\varepsilon_1$, $\varepsilon_2$. A morphism $\nu_2$ from $D_k$ to $D_2$ over $k$ is given; `hν₂` pins it down by requiring, for every $k$-scheme $t$ and every $t$-point $a$ of $D_k$, an isomorphism between the bundle classified by $a$ followed by $\nu_2$ and the rigidification (in the sense of `Scheme.Modules.rigidify`, with respect to `rigSection c₂ t ε₂` and the projection $\operatorname{pullback} c_2\, t \to T$) of the pullback along `curveChange i₂.1 i₂.2 t` of the bundle classified by $a$; thus $\nu_2$ is restriction of line bundles to $C_2$.
--
--   **Generic fibre and the geometric curve.** Compatible $\mathbb{Q}$-embeddings of $A$ and $L$ into $\overline{\mathbb{Q}} := \operatorname{AlgebraicClosure} \mathbb{Q}$ are fixed. The base change of $x$ to $L$ is assumed smooth of relative dimension $1$ (`hsmL`) and geometrically integral (`hgiL`), and the base change of $D.\mathrm{toBase}$ to $L$ proper (`hprL`) and geometrically connected (`hgcL`). $M\eta$ is a `CurveModel` over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ for [`ModularCurve.x1FunctionFieldBar (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L182) — a proper smooth integral curve with a prescribed isomorphism of its function field with that field and a bijection between its closed points and places — and $e\eta$ is an isomorphism of $M\eta.C$ with the fibre of $x$ over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ compatible with the structure morphisms (`heη`). The preimage in $M\eta.C$ of the open image of the finite chart is nonempty, and `hMηpin` pins the identification on $q$-expansions: for every $a$ in `chartAlgFin A K j`, the element of `x1FunctionFieldBar (M * p)` obtained from $a$ by pulling back along $e\eta$ followed by the first projection, taking the germ on that open set and transporting through the function-field isomorphism, has Laurent expansion [`ModularCurve.coeffMap (algebraMap L ℚ̄)`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L16) of the Laurent expansion of $a$. The hypothesis `hgal` asserts Galois equivariance of the point–place dictionary: for every $g \in \operatorname{Aut}_{\mathbb{Q}}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}})$ fixing the image of $L$ pointwise and all $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points $x, x'$ of $M\eta.C$ such that $x'$ into the finite chart equals $\operatorname{Spec} g$ followed by $x$ into the finite chart, one has `Mη.pointEquivPlace x' = ModularCurve.arithmeticGalois (x1FunctionField (M*p)) g • Mη.pointEquivPlace x`. Finally `hin` is [`ModularCurve.HeckeDiamondInputsAll (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1HeckeModule.html#L58) (for each prime $\ell$ the predicate `HeckeInputsOneAlong ℚ̄ (M*p) ℓ`, and for each $d$ coprime to $M p$ the existence of a diamond automorphism of `x1FunctionField (M*p)` and of a base-change automorphism of `x1FunctionFieldBar (M*p)` over it) and `hcomm` is [`ModularCurve.HeckeDiamondCommuteBar (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1HeckeModule.html#L54), the pairwise commutation of the generators `heckeDiamondGenBar`.
--
--   **Galois action on the base ring.** A multiplicative semiring action of $\operatorname{Gal}(L/\mathbb{Q}) = L \simeq_{\mathbb{Q}} L$ on $A$ is given, with `hΓA` asserting that $A \to L$ is equivariant.
--
--   **Group-theoretic dictionaries for the special fibre.** $G$ is a [`ModularCurve.JOneP.NeronSpecialFibreGeom p`](def/ModularCurve_JOnePGeom.html#L9): abelian groups $G.J0s$, $G.JI$, $G.JE$, a subgroup $G.\mathrm{torus}$ of $G.J0s$ and a surjective homomorphism $G.\mathrm{proj} : G.J0s \to G.JI \times G.JE$ with kernel $G.\mathrm{torus}$. Bijections `pts`, `ptsI`, `ptsE` identify $G.J0s$, $G.JI$, $G.JE$ with the $k$-points of $D_k$, $D_1$, $D_2$ respectively; `hadd`, `haddI`, `haddE` state that these bijections are additive in the sense that the bundle classified by the image of a sum is isomorphic to the tensor product of the bundles classified by the images; and `hproj` states that for every $x \in G.J0s$ the first component of $G.\mathrm{proj}\,x$ corresponds to $x$ followed by `RepresentsRelSubPic.pullbackHom i₁.1 i₁.2 hε₁ hreps hrep₁.some` (the restriction homomorphism $D_k \to D_1$ classifying the pullback of the Poincaré bundle along $i_1$) and the second to $x$ followed by $\nu_2$.
--
--   **Hecke and Galois operators on $D$.** A bijection `gpts` identifies [`ModularCurve.JOne (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L186) $= \operatorname{Pic}^0$ of `x1FunctionFieldBar (M*p)` with the $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points of $D.\mathrm{toBase}$; $\varphi$ assigns to each element of [`ModularCurve.HeckeAlgOne`](def/ModularCurve_X1HeckeModule.html#L16) $=$ `MvPolynomial (Nat.Primes ⊕ ℕ) ℤ` an endomorphism of $D$ over $\operatorname{Spec} A$, and $\tau$ assigns to each $s \in \operatorname{Gal}(L/\mathbb{Q})$ an endomorphism of $D.P$ over the twist of $D.\mathrm{toBase}$ by the action of $s$ on $A$. The hypotheses are: `hφmul`, each $\varphi(t)$ is additive for the relative group law attached to `hrep.some` and `algEquivZeroGroupCut`; `hφpts`, for the module structure [`ModularCurve.heckeModuleOneBar (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1HeckeModule.html#L129), `gpts (t • x)` equals `gpts x` followed by $\varphi(t)$; `hτ1` and `hτmul`, $\tau(1)$ is the identity of $D.P$ and $\tau(ss')$ is $\tau(s)$ followed by $\tau(s')$; `hτφ`, each $\tau(s)$ commutes with each $\varphi(t)$; `hgadd`, `gpts` is additive for the relative group law; and `hτpts`, for $\sigma' \in \operatorname{Aut}_{\mathbb{Q}}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}})$ and $s \in \operatorname{Gal}(L/\mathbb{Q})$ with $\sigma' \circ (L \to \overline{\mathbb{Q}}) = (L \to \overline{\mathbb{Q}}) \circ s$, the point `gpts (σ' • x)` is $\operatorname{Spec} \sigma'$ followed by `gpts x` followed by $\tau(s^{-1})$.
--
--   **Abel–Jacobi pinning.** The hypothesis `hDL` is representability for the base change $D_L$ with respect to the base change of $\varepsilon$, and `hPL` the corresponding compatibility of its Poincaré bundle with the pullback of that of `hrep.some`. A morphism $ajL$ from the $L$-fibre of $X$ to $D_L$ over $L$ is given with: `hajLε`, the base-changed section composed with $ajL$ is the zero section of $D_L$; and `hajL`, for every field $K'$, every morphism $t : \operatorname{Spec} K' \to \operatorname{Spec} L$ and every $t$-point $x$ of $X_L$, the bundle classified by $x$ followed by $ajL$ is isomorphic to the tensor product of the line bundle of the relative effective Cartier divisor of the point $x$ with the ideal module of the divisor of $t$ followed by the base-changed section — the Abel–Jacobi normalisation $\mathcal{O}(x) \otimes \mathcal{O}(\varepsilon)^{-1}$. A morphism $kL$ from the $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-fibre to the $L$-fibre of $X$ is given, compatible with both projections (`hkL₁`, `hkL₂`); $ajbar : M\eta.C \to D.P$ is required (`hajbar`) to be $e\eta$ followed by $kL$, $ajL$ and the first projection of $D_L$, and to lie over the structure morphisms (`hajbar_over`). A $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-point $\bar\varepsilon$ of $M\eta.C$ is given which maps into the chart as the base change of $\varepsilon$ (`hεbar`) and satisfies $\bar\varepsilon$ followed by $ajbar$ equals the base change of the zero section (`hεbar_aj`). Finally `hpts_aj` pins `gpts` by Abel–Jacobi: for all $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points $x, s$ of $M\eta.C$ with $s$ lying over $\varepsilon$ in the chart, there is a degree-zero divisor $Dv$ equal to $[\,\mathrm{place}(x)\,] - [\,\mathrm{place}(s)\,]$ with `gpts (Pic0.mk Dv)` equal to $x$ followed by $ajbar$.
--
--   **Igusa identification of $C_1$, and the section.** $w$ is an [`ModularCurve.IntegralWeightOneForm k M`](def/ModularCurve_IgusaFunctionFieldX1.html#L16) (a weight one modular form for $\Gamma_1(M)$ together with an integral $q$-expansion whose reduction over $k$ is nonzero), $Mdl_1$ is a curve model over $k$ for the Igusa function field [`ModularCurve.igusaFunctionFieldX1C k M w`](def/ModularCurve_IgusaFunctionFieldX1.html#L35), and $e_1 : Mdl_1.C \cong C_1$ is an isomorphism over $k$ (`he₁`); the relevant preimage of the finite chart in $Mdl_1.C$ is nonempty. The Gauss-reading hypothesis `hgauss₁` states: for every $a$ in `chartAlgFin A K j` and all power series $x, y$ over $A$ with $\bar y \ne 0$ over $k$, if $a \cdot y = x$ in $L((q))$, then the function on $Mdl_1$ obtained from $a$ by pulling back along $e_1$, $i_1$ and the first projection and transporting through the function-field isomorphism has Laurent expansion $\bar x / \bar y$ over $k$. The hypothesis `hεC₂` states that no point of the reduction of $\varepsilon$ lies in the image of $i_2$. The hypothesis `hεgal` states that $\varepsilon$ is Galois equivariant in the following sense: for every $s \in \operatorname{Gal}(L/\mathbb{Q})$, every endomorphism $ws$ of the two-chart model lying over the twist of $x$ by $s$, and every ring automorphism $\rho_s$ of `chartAlgFin A K j` acting on Laurent expansions by [`ModularCurve.coeffMap`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L16) of $s$, if the finite chart inclusion followed by $ws$ equals $\operatorname{Spec} \rho_s$ followed by the finite chart inclusion, then $\varepsilon$ followed by $ws$ equals $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the action of $s$ on $A$ followed by $\varepsilon$.
--
--   **Conclusion.** For every $s \in \operatorname{Gal}(L/\mathbb{Q})$ and all $k$-points $v, v'$ of the special fibre $D_k$ (sections of $(D_k).\mathrm{toBase}$ over the identity of $\operatorname{Spec} k$), if
--
--   (i) $v'$ followed by the first projection $D_k \to D.P$ equals $v$ followed by that projection and then by $\tau(s)$,
--
--   (ii) $v$ followed by the restriction homomorphism `RepresentsRelSubPic.pullbackHom i₁.1 i₁.2 hε₁ hreps hrep₁.some` to $D_1$ equals the unit section of the relative group law on $D_1.\mathrm{toBase}$ attached to `hrep₁.some` at the identity of $\operatorname{Spec} k$, and
--
--   (iii) $v$ followed by $\nu_2$ equals the unit section of the relative group law on $D_2.\mathrm{toBase}$ attached to `hrep₂.some` at the identity of $\operatorname{Spec} k$,
--
--   then $v' = v$.
--
--   In other words, the automorphism of the special fibre induced by $\tau(s)$ fixes every $k$-point of $D_k$ annihilated by both component projections, i.e. every point of the kernel $\ker(D_k \to D_1 \times D_2)$.
--
--   This is the statement that $\operatorname{Gal}(\mathbb{Q}(\zeta_p)/\mathbb{Q})$ acts trivially on the toric part of the special fibre at $p$ of the rigidified relative $\operatorname{Pic}^0$ of the two-chart model of $X_1(Mp)$, the group scheme analogue of Edixhoven's observation that Galois and the diamond operators act trivially on the character group of the torus attached to the two components of the reduction. It feeds the assembly of the special-fibre operator data for $J_1(Mp)$ used in the level-lowering argument.
